<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The June 12 high reflected peak geopolitical risk premium and pre Warsh rate expectations. The subsequent decline tracks a stronger US dollar and the US Iran peace roadmap signed in Switzerland.</p>
',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who bought gold or energy exposure near the spring highs feel a mix of regret and confusion. They are not panicked, because the decline has been gradual, but they are anchored on the peak and experiencing it as a loss relative to that number rather than as a market correctly repricing reduced risk.</p>
<p>Clients with no direct exposure but who follow headlines are quietly recalibrating what they think gold or oil "should" cost, and may delay decisions waiting for a return to the anchor that is unlikely to come on the original timeline.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Gold and oil have both pulled back from their June highs, and I want to walk through why before you read too much into it. Gold touched just over 4,280 dollars an ounce in mid June, when the market was pricing real geopolitical risk and uncertainty about Fed policy under a brand new chair. It is trading closer to 4,075 today. That is not gold becoming less valuable. It is the market removing a risk premium that reflected a specific, now easing situation: the Hormuz disruption and the uncertainty before Chair Warsh''s first meeting.<br><br>The same logic applies to oil. WTI is back near where it traded before the conflict began in February, because the conflict that drove it up is the thing that is now resolving. If you are looking at either of these prices against where they were eight weeks ago, you are comparing today''s number to a number that was set by conditions that no longer apply. The more useful comparison is to where they were before the disruption started, because that is closer to what the underlying fundamentals actually support right now.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who added to gold or energy positions specifically during the May or June run up, who are most likely to be anchored on their entry point and the broader market peak simultaneously.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with long standing, pre conflict gold or energy allocations as part of a diversified portfolio, who have not changed their holdings but may still ask why the "story" seems to be reversing.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients sitting on cash who were waiting for a pullback before adding gold or energy exposure, for whom the current level may represent a more rational entry point than the one they were anchored on.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client accounts opened or added to during the May to mid June window for gold ETFs, bullion proxies, or energy sector concentration</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag any client conversation in the past two weeks that referenced the mid June price level as a benchmark</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a short, dated note on the pre conflict reference levels for gold and WTI to use as the replacement anchor in conversations</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document any client decision to hold or add at current levels with the rationale discussed, separate from the prior anchor</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> A quick note on gold and oil this week<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following our call, I wanted to put the gold and oil moves in writing. Gold is down from its mid June peak near 4,280 US dollars to around 4,075 today, and WTI crude has slipped back toward levels last seen before the Strait of Hormuz disruption began in February.<br><br>
The decline reflects a real and explainable shift: a 60 day US Iran peace roadmap easing supply risk, and a stronger US dollar following the Fed''s first policy meeting under Chair Warsh. Neither move suggests the underlying assets are worth less. It suggests the risk premium that built up earlier this year is coming back out.<br><br>
If you would like to talk through what this means for your specific allocation, I am happy to set up a call this week.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding gold or energy ETFs bought near the spring highs:</strong> likely anchored on entry prices with no professional context for the current pullback, and at risk of either panic selling or doubling down at the wrong moment.</p>
<p><strong>Self directed retirees with commodity heavy portfolios:</strong> often built these positions specifically as a geopolitical hedge and have no framework for what to do once the hedge''s reason for existing starts to fade.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I noticed gold has pulled back more than ten percent from its June high. I work with a number of clients who hold gold or energy positions from earlier this year, and most of them have questions right now about whether to hold, add, or trim. Do you have a few minutes to talk through where things stand?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A DIY investor watching gold and oil retrace from their 2026 highs has no professional framework for separating a real fundamental shift from short term noise. They are reading the decline against the peak they remember, not against the pre conflict baseline that better reflects fair value once the geopolitical premium unwinds.</p>
<p>An advisor''s value here is not predicting where gold or oil go next. It is correcting the reference point the client is using to evaluate the move at all, which is the difference between a client who makes a calm decision and one who reacts to a number that was never the right benchmark.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>When did you first add gold or energy exposure to your portfolio, and what was the reasoning at the time?</p>
<p>Have you looked at where gold or oil traded before this year''s conflict began, or mainly at the highs from the past two months?</p>
<p>If gold or oil kept falling from here, would you know what level you would consider attractive, or are you watching for a return to the recent peak?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through how the Fed''s new chair and the easing Hormuz situation are connected to these price moves?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Gold''s pullback and what it actually means<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
Gold has fallen more than ten percent from its mid June peak, and WTI crude is back near levels last seen before this year''s Middle East disruption began. If you are holding either, it is worth understanding why before deciding what to do next.<br><br>
I help clients separate a genuine shift in fundamentals from a market simply giving back a risk premium that built up earlier this year. If you would like a second perspective on your position, I would be glad to set up a short call.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
