  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with non registered investments and a lower income spouse or adult child often feel they missed the window on income splitting strategies, assuming rates only get worse from here. Business owner clients with CCPC retained earnings are anxious about passive income rules eating into their small business deduction and are not always aware a prescribed rate loan is one of the few levers still available to them.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The CRA just confirmed the prescribed rate stays at 3 percent for another quarter, and that is actually good news for anyone considering an income splitting loan. Here is why timing matters: once you set up a prescribed rate loan, the rate is locked for as long as the loan is outstanding, even if the CRA''s rate goes up later. Right now, government bond yields have been climbing because of this year''s inflation pressure and the new Fed chair''s hawkish stance, but the CRA''s formula, which is based on shorter term Treasury bill yields, has stayed at 3 percent for five quarters running.<br><br>That gap between the locked in rate and what your investments can reasonably be expected to earn is the entire mechanism behind this strategy. The lower the rate you lock in, the lower the bar your investments need to clear for the income splitting to pay off, and you keep that low rate for the life of the loan. If we are going to do this, doing it before the next quarterly reset is the only way to lock in this quarter''s number.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Couples with a significant income gap and meaningful non registered assets, where one spouse is in a materially lower tax bracket and has investment capacity.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Incorporated business owners whose corporation holds retained earnings as passive investments, where the strategy requires a trust structure and more careful documentation.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients who assumed the planning window had closed when rates rose in 2024, who may not realize the rate has come back down and stabilized at a level that makes the strategy attractive again.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with a spousal or family income gap and non registered investable assets above a meaningful threshold</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Confirm whether any CCPC clients are approaching the 50,000 dollar passive income threshold that erodes the small business deduction</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Coordinate with each client''s tax advisor on loan documentation before the end of the current quarter</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Set a calendar reminder for the January 30 annual interest payment deadline on any loan funded this quarter</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The prescribed rate held at 3 percent, here is why that matters<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The CRA confirmed this week that the prescribed rate for family loans stays at 3 percent through September. Given that government bond yields have been climbing this year, this is a wider gap than it has been in some time, and it is the gap that makes a prescribed rate loan worth considering for income splitting.<br><br>
Because the rate locks in for the life of the loan, setting one up this quarter secures today''s 3 percent rather than whatever the rate happens to be when the CRA next reviews it in the fall.<br><br>
If you would like to discuss whether this fits your situation, let''s set up a time this week.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investing couples with an income gap:</strong> self directed investors who have never structured a prescribed rate loan because no one has explained the mechanism or the timing pressure created by the quarterly reset.</p>
<p><strong>Incorporated professionals and small business owners:</strong> doctors, lawyers, and consultants with CCPC retained earnings who are getting squeezed by the passive income rules and may not know a prescribed rate loan to a spouse or family trust is one of their few remaining levers.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">The CRA just confirmed the prescribed rate stays at 3 percent for another quarter, which is actually a meaningful planning window given where bond yields have moved this year. Do you currently have any income splitting strategy in place between you and your spouse, or is that something nobody has walked you through?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A DIY investor or business owner managing their own portfolio rarely tracks the CRA''s quarterly prescribed rate announcements, and even less often understands that the rate locks in for the life of a loan once it is set up. They are leaving a specific, quantifiable tax planning opportunity on the table simply because no one flagged the timing.</p>
<p>The advisor''s value here is concrete: identifying the income gap, calculating whether the strategy clears its hurdle rate, and coordinating the documentation before the quarter closes. This is not a generic pitch about tax efficiency. It is a dated opportunity with a real deadline.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you and your spouse have a meaningful gap in your marginal tax rates?</p>
<p>Do you hold non registered investments, either personally or inside a corporation, beyond what fits in your RRSP and TFSA?</p>
<p>Has anyone explained how a prescribed rate loan works, or how the rate you lock in today stays fixed even if rates rise later?</p>
<p>If you own a corporation, do you know how close your passive investment income is to the threshold that reduces your small business deduction?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
