<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Daily transit figures before June 17 are estimated from reported near total halt conditions. The central shipping channel remains closed pending demining, with traffic routed through narrower northern and southern alternatives.</p>
',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with energy exposure feel relief that the worst of the conflict appears behind markets, but that relief is layered with a quiet uncertainty about whether to trust the calm given how abruptly this year''s volatility began. Clients without direct energy exposure mostly feel the story has concluded and are not tracking the 60 day window at all.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The peace agreement that reopened the Strait of Hormuz is real, and oil prices have responded to it appropriately. But there is a detail in the agreement worth knowing: Iran''s commitment to free passage through the strait runs for sixty days from when it was signed in mid June, which puts the expiry in mid August. After that, the strait''s longer term status depends on a final deal that still has to work through Iran''s nuclear program, the single hardest issue on the table.<br><br>I am not telling you this to suggest the deal is at risk of falling apart. The economic incentives on both sides point toward getting a final agreement done. I am telling you because oil prices have moved as though the entire risk is resolved, when what has actually happened is a serious, promising, but still incomplete de escalation. If your energy positioning assumes the story is finished, it is worth understanding that there is still a real date on the calendar that matters.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated positions in Canadian energy producers who have been told, implicitly or explicitly, that the de escalation trade is complete and there is nothing more to watch.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with diversified portfolios holding energy as one sector among several, where the tail risk matters for sector weighting decisions but not for the broader plan.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients positioned in lower breakeven, lower leverage energy producers who are better suited to a renewed bout of headline driven volatility than higher cost producers priced for permanent calm.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Mark mid August on the calendar as the expiry of Iran''s current free passage commitment through Hormuz</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy sector concentration in client portfolios for balance sheet quality and breakeven cost, not just commodity price exposure</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag any client conversation that treated the Hormuz situation as fully and permanently resolved</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Watch for news from rescheduled Switzerland talks as the next concrete data point on negotiation progress</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Hormuz deal has a clock on it<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following our conversation, I wanted to flag one detail in the US Iran agreement that reopened the Strait of Hormuz. Iran''s commitment to free passage runs sixty days from the June 17 signing, putting the expiry in mid August, with a final, longer term arrangement still dependent on resolving Iran''s nuclear program.<br><br>
Oil prices have settled back toward pre conflict levels, which reflects real progress. It does not yet reflect a fully closed chapter. I will be watching the rescheduled talks in Switzerland as the next signal on how this proceeds, and will keep you posted.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors holding Canadian energy names bought during the conflict:</strong> self directed investors who added exposure during the volatility earlier this year and have not revisited the position now that the headline story appears resolved.</p>
<p><strong>Investors who exited energy entirely on the peace deal headline:</strong> those who took the deal as a clean signal to rotate out, without understanding the conditional, time limited nature of what was actually signed.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Most people think the Hormuz situation is fully resolved now that oil prices have come back down, but the agreement that reopened the strait actually expires in mid August unless a final deal replaces it. Have you taken another look at your energy positioning since the peace deal was signed, or is it still set up the way it was during the conflict?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A DIY investor reacting to the peace deal headline is making a binary decision, in or out, based on a story the market has already priced as fully resolved. What they are missing is the specific structure of the agreement: a sixty day window with a real expiry date, still dependent on resolving the hardest issue in the negotiation.</p>
<p>An advisor''s value is distinguishing the base case, continued gradual normalization, from the tail risk, a stalled negotiation that lets the current arrangement lapse without a replacement, and positioning a client''s energy exposure for either outcome rather than betting the story is finished.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Did you change your energy sector positioning when the US Iran peace deal was announced, and if so, how?</p>
<p>Are you aware that the current free passage agreement through the Strait of Hormuz has a sixty day window that expires in mid August?</p>
<p>How would you want your portfolio positioned if oil market volatility picked back up later this summer?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through the difference between Canadian energy producers with low breakeven costs versus those more exposed to a renewed price swing?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Hormuz peace deal has an expiry date<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
Oil prices have settled back toward pre conflict levels following the US Iran peace agreement, but the deal''s actual commitment on Strait of Hormuz traffic runs only sixty days from its June 17 signing. A final, longer term arrangement still depends on resolving Iran''s nuclear program, the most contentious issue in the talks.<br><br>
If your energy positioning was set during this year''s conflict, or adjusted when the peace deal was announced, it may be worth a second look before that window closes in mid August.<br><br>
I would be glad to walk through what this means for your portfolio.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
